{"id": "clm:9ba0caee161999a6d917582e93d50f01d34328a90ad0d625f07bc345e7e1e3e3", "type": "statement", "text": "Choose snapshot and ongoing-change behavior explicitly. Debezium documents initial snapshots followed by streaming and separately configurable snapshot modes; the Spark source documents latest or timestamp startup when no offset exists. These are different bootstrap contracts, so switching tools requires a backfill-and-handoff design. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-10-23)", "source_url": "https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/mongodb.html", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/spark-connector/current/streaming-mode/streaming-read-config.md"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:9506a3eb9b6c2c62d4ac422a5f63e01de7799010628da3faf85da4dfa113e966", "ev:bfc5179983668eeaabf8383ef34cbe96d3046dd5c23ac819c25d930a7384c5da"]}
{"id": "clm:4c900bbceecbbfa0fa3c460556f5910db176682c0da7e2d48a9686a12c2f243f", "type": "statement", "text": "Full-document output is not sufficient evidence of faithful CDC replication. The Spark source filters events without fullDocument when change.stream.publish.full.document.only is enabled; Debezium separately models delete and tombstone events. Compare delete propagation before selecting either data path. (medium confidence) (verified as of 2026-10-23)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/spark-connector/current/streaming-mode/streaming-read-config.md", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "mechanism", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/mongodb.html"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:15448e2bfd1f5b935d5230acef4e110e64c9e408de9e11b8218d27a41a766460", "ev:3b98d0dcf6f38f32d59e9bbf6cbdf6532ba390bcedd00bd5558f684254220a5d"]}
{"id": "clm:ef34a085c8ef3563230d793c4c7c0d1dc395c09f21a2b81a6fd07909292dfb1b", "type": "statement", "text": "Use the MongoDB Spark Connector when a job needs to read MongoDB documents into Spark transformations and write results back; both MongoDB's guide and Databricks' MongoDB examples expose direct DataFrame reads and writes. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/docs/spark-connector/current/getting-started.md", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "how-to", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/spark-data-sources"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:29feac055297e6c4b6a168e8ebe26d0638ae88b80fa06657ff77e00110378ea1", "ev:325750f8580f85f67018e7ebdcc873966965b652c2ca157944952a06ef064bd7"]}
{"id": "clm:c71f166fb7ec59061c6a4bbe55a556fcafa1b985e19ed9bced356dfb1cdf3a2c", "type": "statement", "text": "Meroxa's April 4, 2025 benchmark compares Conduit v0.13.2 with the MongoDB connector v0.2.2 against Kafka Connect v7.8.1 with MongoDB's Kafka connector v1.15.0. The tests use MongoDB-to-Kafka snapshot and CDC workloads on a four-vCPU t2.xlarge EC2 host, with Kafka and MongoDB provided via Docker containers. Meroxa reports about a 52% higher Conduit message rate in CDC; it does not benchmark the MongoDB Spark Connector, so the headline cannot rank that connector. (low confidence) (verified as of 2025-04-04)", "source_url": "https://meroxa.com/blog/conduit-makes-mongodb-cdc-52percent-faster-than-kafka-connect/", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "problems", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:51543228495b08d085ab78498c412661da55bf21793f409e31203c5faf0178d7"]}
{"id": "clm:ab8926609682e563789a8a204a36b62a2b9f82809ed3071772a089d947a609bc", "type": "statement", "text": "Kafka is optional for MongoDB-to-Spark streaming: MongoDB documents direct Spark micro-batches as well as a separate MongoDB Kafka source-to-Databricks pipeline. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/how-seamlessly-use-atlas-databricks-lakehouse-together", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.mongodb.com/docs/spark-connector/current/streaming-mode/streaming-read-config.md"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:88ffa0a5b6f66c8d630d48aeec44e8441e9867a0fa65e825197a3055eef58e18", "ev:8ff74e34a3085e2c8a0976113ac32669d03b75cba8a2c694a274c0c2de2a84e9"]}
{"id": "clm:59994c43c4af400e8dd21a8375f55d1d50d6ed5503d0e9d7c62a4360133b2a92", "type": "statement", "text": "Debezium offers an alternative capture boundary: its MongoDB connector records database changes in Kafka topics, and Lydtech's runnable demonstration separates database application writes from Kafka Connect capture. (high confidence)", "source_url": "https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/mongodb.html", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": ["https://www.lydtechconsulting.com/blog/blog-kafka-connect-debezium-mongodb"], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:0d5946ef1075ad03e412f2d4cb647d23867127c1c8237df5486002e2f7be535e", "ev:2e9e7c1a57cf21b68a81b9a4f01b15419e837d25c99d7d9fbd8c8dfb29ebb174"]}
{"id": "clm:ca53aa734c991de3ec8f06ae9e0492acb423f61094ec572a7c8e1caccae7b999", "type": "statement", "text": "MongoDB describes object-storage alternatives: Atlas Data Federation can export a one-time load to S3; a Kafka source plus S3 sink can stage ongoing changes for Databricks Auto Loader. Staging files still requires ingestion into Delta Lake. (low confidence) (verified as of 2025-12-02)", "source_url": "https://www.mongodb.com/company/blog/technical/how-seamlessly-use-atlas-databricks-lakehouse-together", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:591a2f7335fb690258369243ff94c0a331da1931eb609ca4a51b7aa4b33865b7", "ev:e167cfd582540c28a762ada3cfcd2fcf39c630e79dd7bc8ac54589cc9c3782d6", "ev:f71b381ef5cc21761f15ab08d71bdb734fcea3638715b1c28313cbab3b71c321"]}
{"id": "clm:bcfd80b96f2cbbad366b9b7d39d68491597f78621be9f6d1ad4702420f729ec0", "type": "statement", "text": "Databricks distinguishes Spark Data Source API jobs for custom ETL and external writes, federation for governed ad-hoc querying, and managed ingestion for persistent replication. This generic comparison does not establish MongoDB support for every alternative; verify a source-specific support matrix before treating them as substitutes. (low confidence) (verified as of 2026-09-28)", "source_url": "https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/connect/spark-data-sources", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:bba83e0f8ea01f0b7e198d28922fe69809b518d88fd31a63a6c85019e7d81772", "ev:c17b7fe904bb3a975695da40bc277c61a85782e398a32685ce1104dc7282fe0f"]}
{"id": "clm:ace66a07a2e2a1d582d547384ffe05f6e777d69ead20982b6e267626ff60cff1", "type": "statement", "text": "Kafka buffering helps only after changes reach Kafka. Debezium warns that a long capture outage can exhaust MongoDB oplog history and that consumers must handle duplicate events under failures; adding Kafka does not remove source-retention and replay requirements. (low confidence)", "source_url": "https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/connectors/mongodb.html", "anchor": "", "page_class": "reference", "keywords": ["Spark Connector vs Alternatives"], "code": null, "origin": "dr", "section": "facts", "relation": "about", "score": 1.0, "also": [], "semantic_score": null, "pass": "keyword", "canonical_concept_id": "concept:2a6f7ae86aed855f93bebd8b0846359d029e760154162f616331462f821d561e", "validation_status": "SUPPORTED", "evidence_ids": ["ev:cc3ecf2a8e5f1f6defda6662b3d683aed85b71400e3a8c520d937890bb4d7fe2"]}
